      "source_example_id": "e-10994",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns",
      "kp_id": "e-10994"
    },
    "q-82192": {
      "dom_id": "step-q82192",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find $7det(A)-2det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -0.5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\ndet(B)=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7det(A)-2det(B)=7\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-6)=12.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
